[{:db/id -1, :doc/id "doc-ayatori-bench-coscientist-iteration-12", :doc/title "ayatori Co-Scientist Iteration 12 — cutting an ordering into K packs is not partition alignment, and the judge said so", :doc/path "bench/coscientist-iteration-12.edn", :doc/body "# Co-Scientist iteration 12 - cutting an ordering into K packs is not partition alignment, and the judge said so\n\n> Seed: iteration 11's H2 (root ADR-2609119000): packs aligned to the index's\n> unit -- one prolly internal node's siblings per pack -- make a range query\n> open few packs. Judge: `bench/fitness` under `bench/benchmark.edn` (fixed\n> 2026-09-11): reads and bytes at 800 subjects, packs opened over packs\n> total, the 3-row equality gate.\n\n## Generate (prediction, before running)\n\nThe measurable form chosen: the SAME 23 blocks cut into K contiguous chunks\nof an ordering (K = 2, 4, 8), each chunk a CARv2 pack, a CID -> pack catalog,\npacks opened lazily by the first read that needs them, the 64 KiB window\nreader from iteration 11 inside each. Two orderings: breadth-first from the\nroot (siblings adjacent) and hash order.\n\n  Predicted: bfs opens a small, K-insensitive number of packs (the\n  traversal's blocks sit in adjacent chunks); hash opens min(K, blocks\n  needed). Reads track packs opened for bfs.\n\n## Rank -- what the judge returned\n\n    subj   K |  multi/hash  opened  reads   bytes |  multi/bfs  opened  reads   bytes\n     800   2                  2/2      6  237234                2/2      6  263124\n     800   4                  3/4      6  249844                3/4      7  265402\n     800   8                  5/8      7  256074                4/8      8  265302\n\n    reference at 800: per-object 12 / 109764   exact 8 / 110996\n    iteration 11 single pack, window, bfs:      4 / 197558\n\n## Reflect -- REFUTED as formulated\n\n  - bfs opened as many packs as hash at K=2 and K=4, and one fewer at K=8\n    (4 vs 5) while doing MORE reads (8 vs 7) and moving more bytes. The\n    predicted K-insensitivity did not appear: opened grew 2 -> 3 -> 4 with K.\n  - Every multi-pack arm is worse than iteration 11's single pack on reads\n    (6..8 against 4) and worse than the exact reader on bytes.\n\nWhy, read off the numbers rather than assumed: the query's six blocks are a\nroot, internal nodes of two indexes, and leaves -- they span LEVELS of the\nprolly tree. Breadth-first order puts levels in sequence, so cutting it into\nequal-COUNT chunks puts a level boundary, not a sibling boundary, at most\ncuts; the traversal then crosses chunks exactly as often as it crosses\nlevels. Adjacency survived in iteration 11 because one pack held everything\nand the window could run across what became chunk boundaries here. And the\nchunks are equal in count, not bytes: a chunk of three large leaves exceeds\nthe window, so one opened pack costs head + index + a window forward -- which\nis why bfs at K=8 opened fewer packs and still read more.\n\nSo \"index ごとに pack\" is right and this was not it. Partition alignment\nmeans cutting at the tree's OWN boundaries -- the children of one internal\nnode, whatever their count and bytes -- and that needs the node structure at\nwrite time, which this harness's ordering functions do not have.\n\nWhat did hold, trivially: the catalog pruned. At K=8 only 4-5 of 8 packs\nwere opened by either ordering, so a CID -> pack map is already a filter;\nH3's stats would decide which of the opened ones could have stayed closed.\n\n## Meta\n\nVerdict under bench/benchmark.edn: NO GROWTH. Best multi-pack arm at the\nfixed shape is hash K=2 at 6 reads / 237 KB, behind iteration 11's 4 reads /\n198 KB on both axes. Recorded on the scoreboard as such.\n\nThe loop's value this iteration is the refutation: a plausible proxy for the\nlake design (\"cut BFS into K\") measures as a regression, and the reason\nnames the missing input (node boundaries). Without the judge this would\nhave shipped as \"packs are now partitioned\".\n\nSeed for iteration 13:\n\n  - H2': cut packs at prolly internal-node boundaries. Needs `arrangement`\n    or `prolly-tree` to expose, at commit time, which CIDs are one node's\n    children -- a write-side seam, not a reader change. Predicted: opened =\n    1 + number of leaf groups the range spans; reads = opened.\n  - The chunking-by-count artefact: any partition policy must bound BYTES\n    per pack to the window, or an opened pack costs three reads instead of\n    one. That is a constraint on the writer, now measured.\n  - H3 stays: with H2' in place, count packs opened that contributed no\n    block to the answer; that number is what catalog stats would remove.\n"}]
